1
00:00:00,001 --> 00:00:05,000
They save lives, find murderers,

2
00:00:05,001 --> 00:00:09,000
and teach a new generation how the human body works.

3
00:00:09,001 --> 00:00:15,000
They can be buried, embalmed, donated, and cremated.

4
00:00:15,001 --> 00:00:18,000
They can even create a new habitat.

5
00:00:18,001 --> 00:00:24,000
If you work hard in life, just wait until you see what you can do after you're dead.

6
00:00:24,001 --> 00:00:29,000
Now, Corpse Tech on Modern Marvels.

7
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:44,000
What happens after we die?

8
00:00:45,980 --> 00:00:49,660
This simple question has fueled wars, mass fear,

9
00:00:50,080 --> 00:00:52,980
and great debate since man first walked the earth.

10
00:00:54,580 --> 00:00:57,820
But it's the living, not the dead, that raise all the ruckus.

11
00:00:57,821 --> 00:01:00,760
The dead are dead.

12
00:01:01,320 --> 00:01:02,580
You can't help them.

13
00:01:03,880 --> 00:01:07,720
You can't help them, but maybe they can help you.

14
00:01:09,360 --> 00:01:11,300
They might even help catch a killer.

15
00:01:12,080 --> 00:01:19,620
I know that we have helped solve several cases by the research we have performed here.

16
00:01:20,980 --> 00:01:27,800
At the University of Tennessee, a unique and what some might call creepy forensic research facility exists.

17
00:01:27,820 --> 00:01:30,760
to answer questions few dare to ask.

18
00:01:33,900 --> 00:01:37,220
Behind a locked fence laid dozens of corpses

19
00:01:37,221 --> 00:01:39,040
in varying stages of decay.

20
00:01:40,660 --> 00:01:43,600
It's known to some as the body farm.

21
00:01:44,660 --> 00:01:47,280
This one-of-a-kind facility covers almost three acres.

22
00:01:48,480 --> 00:01:52,160
So you're right now at the Anthropological Research Facility,

23
00:01:52,400 --> 00:01:55,760
which is a part of the Forensic Anthropology Center

24
00:01:55,761 --> 00:01:57,160
here at the University of Tennessee.

25
00:01:57,820 --> 00:02:01,120
We started this facility in 1981.

26
00:02:01,960 --> 00:02:05,620
The main focus of our existence is to study human decomposition.

27
00:02:07,100 --> 00:02:10,040
Under each of these plastic tarps is a body.

28
00:02:11,460 --> 00:02:13,240
But some also lay exposed.

29
00:02:14,000 --> 00:02:15,240
Some are placed in cars.

30
00:02:15,960 --> 00:02:17,540
And some are hidden underground.

31
00:02:18,640 --> 00:02:21,320
More than 150 populate the property.

32
00:02:22,020 --> 00:02:24,320
Each has been donated for the purpose of study.

33
00:02:24,321 --> 00:02:31,160
And as it turns out, the dead can weave long and intricate tales for trained, receptive experts.

34
00:02:33,560 --> 00:02:36,660
It's extremely important to study human decomposition.

35
00:02:37,360 --> 00:02:42,360
One of the major things that people ask for is, how long has someone been dead?

36
00:02:42,860 --> 00:02:45,740
What is that post-mortem interval, as we call it?

37
00:02:46,860 --> 00:02:51,080
But the length of time a person has been dead isn't that easy to establish.

38
00:02:51,081 --> 00:02:58,440
The time since death is very important because perpetrators typically have an alibi.

39
00:02:58,441 --> 00:03:03,500
If you can determine that they don't have an alibi at the time when the time since death

40
00:03:03,501 --> 00:03:07,700
has been determined for an individual, you could possibly pin the perpetrator to the

41
00:03:07,701 --> 00:03:08,700
crime.

42
00:03:08,701 --> 00:03:15,880
There are four major stages of decomposition that human bodies undergo.

43
00:03:15,881 --> 00:03:21,200
The first is the fresh, or autolysis stage, in which cells and organs begin to chemically

44
00:03:21,201 --> 00:03:25,100
break down.

45
00:03:25,101 --> 00:03:30,220
That breakdown generates the growth of bacteria that eats away at the soft tissue, generating

46
00:03:30,221 --> 00:03:34,640
gases which begin to bloat the body.

47
00:03:34,641 --> 00:03:39,260
That bloating, or putrefaction, is stage two.

48
00:03:39,261 --> 00:03:45,640
The third stage is decay, when the body eventually ruptures and all liquids and gases escape.

49
00:03:45,641 --> 00:03:50,000
At the end of the third stage, only the bones remain.

50
00:03:51,440 --> 00:03:58,620
The final stage is skeletonization, the deterioration of the bones themselves, which will take thousands of years.

51
00:04:03,340 --> 00:04:10,700
This donation is what we consider advanced decomposition or initial skeletonization.

52
00:04:10,701 --> 00:04:15,160
One of the first things that becomes skeletal is the skull.

53
00:04:15,700 --> 00:04:21,560
And a lot of that has to do with the fact that you have more openings in your head and you have less tissue on your head.

54
00:04:21,960 --> 00:04:28,580
And then one of the last things that tends to kind of go away is the torso area, which is right here.

55
00:04:29,140 --> 00:04:33,360
And a lot of that's because you think about how much muscle and how much tissue you have in those regions.

56
00:04:34,800 --> 00:04:40,540
These bodies provide valuable insight for forensic students and officials from all segments of law enforcement.

57
00:04:40,700 --> 00:04:46,380
from local to federal agencies, who come here to learn what a corpse can teach.

58
00:04:47,100 --> 00:04:50,240
This particular one appears to be an end of active decay.

59
00:04:51,380 --> 00:04:54,440
And one of the interesting things about this particular individual

60
00:04:54,441 --> 00:04:58,080
is the formation of what we call adiposea, or grave wax.

61
00:04:58,320 --> 00:05:02,300
It's very cheesy and kind of, you know, gooey material.

62
00:05:02,900 --> 00:05:06,700
By looking at the composition and structure of the adiposea,

63
00:05:06,701 --> 00:05:10,760
determine whether it was a very rapid decompositional process

64
00:05:10,761 --> 00:05:12,240
or if it was very slow.

65
00:05:13,000 --> 00:05:17,280
A rapid decomposition ends up with a very crumbly type of adiposea.

66
00:05:17,640 --> 00:05:20,040
A slow decomposition is much more paste-like.

67
00:05:21,380 --> 00:05:25,480
Insect activity is a key tool in determining a timeline of decomposition.

68
00:05:28,100 --> 00:05:31,340
There's a specific reason the flies lay their eggs on the body

69
00:05:31,341 --> 00:05:33,560
in a certain location, why they hatch.

70
00:05:33,561 --> 00:05:40,100
I mean, it's a very important point to realize that the maggot mass doesn't just appear for no reason.

71
00:05:40,480 --> 00:05:44,440
Knowing all these things is all very important in doing forensic investigations.

72
00:05:46,300 --> 00:05:49,400
A corpse can even tell investigators where it's been.

73
00:05:50,600 --> 00:05:55,980
Here you have what we call true skeletonization, where there isn't any remaining tissue.

74
00:05:56,300 --> 00:06:00,260
You can see every element from the skull to the feet.

75
00:06:00,261 --> 00:06:04,960
This is the top of your head, and you can see it's much lighter in color.

76
00:06:05,680 --> 00:06:10,980
And then you move down and you have very dark colors, and that's because they were covered.

77
00:06:12,040 --> 00:06:16,680
The bones absorb the color of the surrounding soil, making them difficult to see.

78
00:06:18,120 --> 00:06:21,900
So how do you find a corpse that's buried or covered in the middle of nowhere?

79
00:06:23,940 --> 00:06:28,420
My two areas of expertise include determining how long someone's been dead

80
00:06:28,421 --> 00:06:31,180
and doing a clandestine grave detection.

81
00:06:31,720 --> 00:06:34,360
Here we see an example of a clandestine grave.

82
00:06:34,900 --> 00:06:38,360
A fresh grave, which is usually just a couple months old,

83
00:06:38,680 --> 00:06:42,360
will have a nice mound of dirt where the perpetrator refilled the grave

84
00:06:42,361 --> 00:06:43,800
after putting a body in there.

85
00:06:44,320 --> 00:06:49,060
As the decomposition process continues, the soft tissue liquefies

86
00:06:49,061 --> 00:06:52,260
and the ground sinks and subsides.

87
00:06:52,261 --> 00:06:56,760
But as time progresses and this thing fills back in with leaf debris

88
00:06:56,761 --> 00:06:59,380
and sticks and limbs like we see here,

89
00:06:59,540 --> 00:07:01,280
it becomes very, very difficult.

90
00:07:01,880 --> 00:07:03,980
And that's part of my research efforts here,

91
00:07:04,300 --> 00:07:06,820
is trying to determine how to locate these graves

92
00:07:06,821 --> 00:07:09,020
to make the investigator's job much easier.

93
00:07:10,780 --> 00:07:14,380
Arpad Voss also oversees the longest-running study at the facility,

94
00:07:15,500 --> 00:07:17,380
going on since 1990.

95
00:07:18,760 --> 00:07:21,520
In these particular graves, we have a piping system,

96
00:07:21,740 --> 00:07:23,760
and each one of these is at a different depth.

97
00:07:23,761 --> 00:07:26,060
The reason we did this piping system

98
00:07:26,061 --> 00:07:29,660
is so we can watch these volatile compounds called VOCs

99
00:07:29,661 --> 00:07:33,960
as they migrate up from the body through the soil column

100
00:07:33,961 --> 00:07:35,340
to the very surface.

101
00:07:36,600 --> 00:07:38,800
The volatile organic compounds

102
00:07:38,801 --> 00:07:40,740
comprise an unmistakable smell,

103
00:07:41,620 --> 00:07:43,980
the odor produced by a decomposing body.

104
00:07:45,660 --> 00:07:48,220
And this so-called sweet smell of death

105
00:07:48,221 --> 00:07:51,080
is more chemically complex than anyone thought.

106
00:07:53,400 --> 00:07:55,500
Through this study, we've determined

107
00:07:55,501 --> 00:08:00,020
that over 450 compounds are liberated

108
00:08:00,021 --> 00:08:01,660
during the decompositional process.

109
00:08:02,560 --> 00:08:05,320
These are all found at the body.

110
00:08:06,060 --> 00:08:10,840
And as these odors and compounds and gases, so to speak,

111
00:08:10,960 --> 00:08:12,760
migrate up through the soil column,

112
00:08:13,080 --> 00:08:15,580
the list gets winnowed down to about 30

113
00:08:15,581 --> 00:08:17,660
that are really prominent at the surface

114
00:08:17,661 --> 00:08:21,080
and that we've found to be reproducible body to body.

115
00:08:22,260 --> 00:08:23,720
The goal of this study

116
00:08:23,721 --> 00:08:26,800
is to decipher these 450 compounds

117
00:08:26,801 --> 00:08:28,980
and create a sniffing technology

118
00:08:28,981 --> 00:08:31,760
that now only exists in nature.

119
00:08:35,460 --> 00:08:37,880
Human remains detection canines

120
00:08:37,881 --> 00:08:40,420
are absolutely phenomenal in their abilities.

121
00:08:40,960 --> 00:08:43,580
They have such low detection capabilities,

122
00:08:44,040 --> 00:08:46,100
probably better than a lot of the analytical instruments

123
00:08:46,101 --> 00:08:46,940
we have today.

124
00:08:49,880 --> 00:08:51,780
Properly trained canine units

125
00:08:51,781 --> 00:08:54,480
are the most reliable method of finding a hidden grave.

126
00:08:55,620 --> 00:08:59,180
I have some cadaver material in a little plastic container here,

127
00:08:59,340 --> 00:09:02,620
and we're just hiding it, burying it under some leaves and brush

128
00:09:02,621 --> 00:09:05,120
back here inside the woods a little ways.

129
00:09:06,160 --> 00:09:09,500
We know that they do not key on sight, they key on the scent,

130
00:09:10,080 --> 00:09:12,600
but we just like to take a little extra precaution

131
00:09:12,601 --> 00:09:16,600
and burry it out of sight so there's no chance of him seeing it

132
00:09:16,601 --> 00:09:17,740
and cueing from that.

133
00:09:20,660 --> 00:09:21,360
Diesel.

134
00:09:21,780 --> 00:09:27,560
A two-year-old German shepherd is released more than 250 yards away from the concealed container.

135
00:09:41,380 --> 00:09:45,280
In under two and a half minutes, he zeroes in on the scent.

136
00:09:46,200 --> 00:09:47,960
His alert is what we call passive alert.

137
00:09:48,260 --> 00:09:51,420
The first thing he does is he turns around, he eyeballs me, he's very, very hard.

138
00:09:51,780 --> 00:09:53,720
becomes very, very stiff in his actions.

139
00:09:54,180 --> 00:09:56,240
And I know at that point he's in scent,

140
00:09:56,320 --> 00:09:58,020
probably is at the source of the scent.

141
00:09:58,820 --> 00:10:01,240
The only way to properly train these canines

142
00:10:01,241 --> 00:10:04,300
is to use actual human cadaver remains,

143
00:10:04,540 --> 00:10:06,720
which smell like no other animal tissue.

144
00:10:07,540 --> 00:10:10,300
He discriminates decomposing human tissue

145
00:10:10,301 --> 00:10:12,660
from decomposing any kind of other tissue,

146
00:10:13,100 --> 00:10:17,300
whether it's pig, cow, horse, deer, groundhog, anything else.

147
00:10:17,620 --> 00:10:20,040
He'll totally ignore it and just walk right by it.

148
00:10:21,780 --> 00:10:27,660
Arpad Vaz hopes his work at the body farm will yield technology

149
00:10:27,661 --> 00:10:31,660
that will surpass even Diesel's prodigious talent.

150
00:10:31,661 --> 00:10:36,660
And it will all be thanks to the generosity of the dead.

151
00:10:36,661 --> 00:10:40,540
If you look at the types of donors that we attract,

152
00:10:40,541 --> 00:10:44,540
a lot of them are educators, law enforcement,

153
00:10:44,541 --> 00:10:46,580
health care professionals.

154
00:10:46,581 --> 00:10:50,740
And one of the things that they always say is they want to teach

155
00:10:50,741 --> 00:10:56,380
want to teach people beyond their current life.

156
00:10:56,381 --> 00:10:58,840
This land, however, is not the final resting place

157
00:10:58,841 --> 00:11:01,880
for these donations.

158
00:11:01,881 --> 00:11:05,240
Once the decomposition process is complete,

159
00:11:05,241 --> 00:11:08,800
all bones are stored in the Anthropology Center,

160
00:11:08,801 --> 00:11:12,140
housed in the basement of the University football stadium.

161
00:11:15,360 --> 00:11:20,560
Each donation is painstakingly cleaned, boxed, and catalogued.

162
00:11:20,561 --> 00:11:26,140
It has become the most extensive modern collection of human bones for anthropological study in the United States.

163
00:11:29,200 --> 00:11:34,860
Though you might be surprised to find decomposing bodies and boxes of bones on a university campus,

164
00:11:35,600 --> 00:11:38,620
you would expect to find them here.

165
00:11:40,080 --> 00:11:44,120
Welcome to the Dauphin County Coroner's Office in Harrisburg, Pennsylvania,

166
00:11:44,121 --> 00:11:47,800
where 23-year veteran coroner Graham Hetrick

167
00:11:47,801 --> 00:11:50,620
sees an average of 23 corpses a week.

168
00:11:53,220 --> 00:11:55,740
Their first destination is the refrigerator.

169
00:11:57,600 --> 00:11:59,540
Contrary to movie and TV lore,

170
00:12:00,220 --> 00:12:02,920
most modern morgues use walk-in refrigeration units

171
00:12:02,921 --> 00:12:04,280
rather than morgue drawers.

172
00:12:05,580 --> 00:12:08,080
It's simply easier to move a corpse on a gurney

173
00:12:08,081 --> 00:12:09,300
than lift it into a drawer.

174
00:12:10,300 --> 00:12:13,900
In most cases, refrigeration units nowadays

175
00:12:13,901 --> 00:12:19,600
or refrigeration rooms so that gurneys can be moved in and out.

176
00:12:19,700 --> 00:12:21,400
The purpose of a coroner's office

177
00:12:21,500 --> 00:12:24,440
is to investigate and determine the cause of every sudden,

178
00:12:24,540 --> 00:12:29,240
violent, or unusual death that occurs.

179
00:12:29,340 --> 00:12:34,580
This is almost always done by conducting an autopsy.

180
00:12:34,680 --> 00:12:36,620
This is an autopsy table.

181
00:12:36,720 --> 00:12:40,400
It generally is an L like this with a separate area

182
00:12:40,500 --> 00:12:43,000
for dissection and examination.

183
00:12:43,001 --> 00:12:45,740
area for the body to be.

184
00:12:45,840 --> 00:12:50,700
The body is then actually incised, or there is a cut,

185
00:12:50,800 --> 00:12:56,740
a Y cut made, and the body is opened up.

186
00:12:56,840 --> 00:13:00,480
Every organ is examined and described for the record,

187
00:13:00,580 --> 00:13:03,560
each clue studied, photographed, and documented.

188
00:13:06,660 --> 00:13:11,160
The most common tool in an autopsy is the scalpel.

189
00:13:11,161 --> 00:13:15,160
But the most lurid is the striker saw.

190
00:13:15,260 --> 00:13:17,160
I think there's two reasons for that.

191
00:13:17,260 --> 00:13:21,160
One of them is, of course, who would want their head sawed open,

192
00:13:21,260 --> 00:13:24,160
and the second reason is that famous sound.

193
00:13:27,260 --> 00:13:31,160
But perhaps the most important tool is not even surgical.

194
00:13:31,260 --> 00:13:35,160
There is one tool that is pretty much unique

195
00:13:35,260 --> 00:13:38,160
and used more often in the forensic autopsy.

196
00:13:41,160 --> 00:13:42,200
And that is the ruler.

197
00:13:43,360 --> 00:13:50,020
Because what we want to do is we want to take exacting measurements of wounds

198
00:13:50,021 --> 00:13:54,600
and of the location of those wounds on the body.

199
00:13:56,000 --> 00:14:00,340
These measurements provide investigators key facts about the subject's death.

200
00:14:01,220 --> 00:14:04,200
From the angle of a bullet to the last meal consumed,

201
00:14:04,840 --> 00:14:07,980
nothing is insignificant in unraveling the story of the deceased.

202
00:14:07,981 --> 00:14:15,380
My job and the job of any coroner or medical examiner is to speak for the dead.

203
00:14:16,100 --> 00:14:17,640
I want to tell their story.

204
00:14:18,120 --> 00:14:23,860
I don't want to tell the story in light of what the DA wants to hear or the defense wants to hear.

205
00:14:23,920 --> 00:14:28,160
I want to tell an independent story of what the evidence tells us.

206
00:14:28,940 --> 00:14:30,840
That is what good forensics is.

207
00:14:33,000 --> 00:14:37,340
Corpses can tell untold stories, but they can do much more.

208
00:14:37,980 --> 00:14:42,980
They can make the blind see, allow the injured to heal,

209
00:14:42,981 --> 00:14:46,980
and even save lives.

210
00:14:46,981 --> 00:14:51,980
In 1897, anthropologist George Amos Dorsey helped convict

211
00:14:51,981 --> 00:14:54,980
a Chicago sausage maker of murdering his wife.

212
00:14:54,981 --> 00:14:58,980
Though no body was ever found, he identified bone fragments,

213
00:14:58,981 --> 00:15:01,980
lending support to the theory that she had been ground

214
00:15:01,981 --> 00:15:02,980
into sausage.

215
00:15:02,981 --> 00:15:06,980
Corpse Tech will return on Modern Marvels.

216
00:15:07,980 --> 00:15:16,020
When the worst happens and a tragedy occurs,

217
00:15:16,021 --> 00:15:20,460
there just might be something incredible to come from it,

218
00:15:20,461 --> 00:15:24,060
because the dead can save lives.

219
00:15:24,061 --> 00:15:26,540
The purpose of life is to love and be of service.

220
00:15:26,541 --> 00:15:28,340
And what greater way to be of service

221
00:15:28,341 --> 00:15:31,860
than to actually literally give of ourselves?

222
00:15:31,861 --> 00:15:34,500
One deceased organ donor can save the lives

223
00:15:34,501 --> 00:15:35,500
of eight other people.

224
00:15:37,980 --> 00:15:41,880
At any given moment, there are approximately 100,000 people

225
00:15:41,980 --> 00:15:44,280
awaiting an organ transplant in the United States.

226
00:15:47,480 --> 00:15:54,220
Heart, kidneys, lungs, pancreas, liver, and intestines.

227
00:15:54,320 --> 00:15:57,240
Today, we have the ability to transplant almost every vital

228
00:15:57,340 --> 00:16:00,040
organ in the body.

229
00:16:00,140 --> 00:16:03,080
The time between the recovery of the organs

230
00:16:03,180 --> 00:16:05,140
and the transplantation of the organs

231
00:16:05,240 --> 00:16:07,440
is probably the most critical time factor,

232
00:16:07,441 --> 00:16:09,300
because the clock is ticking.

233
00:16:09,400 --> 00:16:13,680
Organs can only stay outside the body for so long.

234
00:16:13,780 --> 00:16:16,020
When a donor meets the strict medical parameters

235
00:16:16,120 --> 00:16:18,760
for organ donation, the recovery agency

236
00:16:18,860 --> 00:16:21,060
schedules an operating room.

237
00:16:21,160 --> 00:16:23,560
And a transplant surgeon recovers viable organs

238
00:16:23,660 --> 00:16:24,660
for transplantation.

239
00:16:28,260 --> 00:16:31,160
One way to help keep an organ viable outside the body

240
00:16:31,260 --> 00:16:32,760
is with a process called perfusion.

241
00:16:32,761 --> 00:16:37,360
conclusion.

242
00:16:37,361 --> 00:16:42,300
Jim Locke has perfused more than 500 kidneys in his tenure as a certified transplant organ

243
00:16:42,301 --> 00:16:47,300
perfusionist.

244
00:16:47,301 --> 00:16:53,140
Kidneys were the first organ to be successfully transplanted over 50 years ago.

245
00:16:53,141 --> 00:16:55,520
And they are perhaps the most durable human organ.

246
00:16:55,521 --> 00:17:02,400
This machine won't make a bad kidney good, but it certainly will.

247
00:17:02,401 --> 00:17:04,200
It's like the army, it'll be all it can be,

248
00:17:04,300 --> 00:17:07,140
and we'll get the information necessary to let us know

249
00:17:07,240 --> 00:17:08,600
if it's going to function immediately,

250
00:17:08,700 --> 00:17:10,300
or if it's going to be delayed, or if it's not

251
00:17:10,400 --> 00:17:11,740
going to work at all.

252
00:17:11,840 --> 00:17:13,740
Ready to go.

253
00:17:13,840 --> 00:17:16,980
But beyond life-giving organs, the dead can also

254
00:17:17,080 --> 00:17:22,080
gift us with tissues that save and heal.

255
00:17:22,180 --> 00:17:25,260
Donated tissue from one corpse can change the lives of more

256
00:17:25,360 --> 00:17:28,820
than 200 people.

257
00:17:28,920 --> 00:17:31,220
When people donate tissue, it really

258
00:17:31,221 --> 00:17:36,260
involves donation of various tissue forms.

259
00:17:36,261 --> 00:17:41,660
Categories of tissue donation include ligaments, tendons,

260
00:17:41,661 --> 00:17:52,200
bone, nerves, skin, veins, heart valves, and even corneas.

261
00:17:52,201 --> 00:17:55,040
LifeNet Health has helped heal more than a million and a half

262
00:17:55,041 --> 00:17:57,280
people through its tissue banking services.

263
00:18:01,220 --> 00:18:03,760
It's one of the top three providers of organ recovery

264
00:18:03,761 --> 00:18:06,320
and tissue banking services in the United States.

265
00:18:08,700 --> 00:18:11,820
Tissue banking differs from organ donation in many ways.

266
00:18:13,180 --> 00:18:17,180
First, unlike organs for transplant, tissue is frozen.

267
00:18:18,400 --> 00:18:20,600
This is one unprocessed donor.

268
00:18:21,180 --> 00:18:23,280
Our freezers here will hold approximately,

269
00:18:23,281 --> 00:18:24,940
about 20 donors per door.

270
00:18:25,100 --> 00:18:26,140
We have 56 doors.

271
00:18:26,500 --> 00:18:29,300
We can store over 1,000 donors at one time.

272
00:18:30,060 --> 00:18:33,540
Each donor is about 28 to 30 pieces of tissue.

273
00:18:34,860 --> 00:18:37,600
And from those 28 to 30 pieces of tissue,

274
00:18:38,680 --> 00:18:42,540
LifeNet Health can create more than 300 different biomedical implants

275
00:18:42,541 --> 00:18:45,260
that can improve the lives of the recipients.

276
00:18:49,380 --> 00:18:51,340
Since its founding in 1982,

277
00:18:52,160 --> 00:18:55,700
LifeNet Health has expanded from three employees to hundreds

278
00:18:55,701 --> 00:18:58,420
while perfecting the system of tissue processing.

279
00:18:59,300 --> 00:19:03,680
We have recovery teams here in Virginia but also work with recovery agencies

280
00:19:03,681 --> 00:19:07,700
throughout the country. They have teams that go and recover the tissue from a

281
00:19:07,701 --> 00:19:12,260
donor and send it here. Once that donor is cleared and released for processing

282
00:19:12,261 --> 00:19:17,080
we bring it back into our certified clean rooms and process clinically

283
00:19:17,081 --> 00:19:21,300
implantable grafts out of that donated tissue.

284
00:19:21,540 --> 00:19:26,220
Preparing human tissue to be used for medical procedures takes patience, time

285
00:19:26,221 --> 00:19:30,220
and a tight system of checks and balances.

286
00:19:30,320 --> 00:19:32,220
Quality is the most important thing we do here.

287
00:19:32,320 --> 00:19:35,220
We have to put up quality tissue grafts

288
00:19:35,320 --> 00:19:36,220
that the surgeons can use.

289
00:19:36,320 --> 00:19:38,760
You can see there's some fiber separation.

290
00:19:38,860 --> 00:19:40,660
We caught it one of our inspection points.

291
00:19:40,760 --> 00:19:43,580
The tendon just started to fray apart a little bit like that.

292
00:19:43,680 --> 00:19:45,580
That will ultimately fail in a patient,

293
00:19:45,680 --> 00:19:48,580
so we can't put that up now.

294
00:19:48,680 --> 00:19:50,820
Two technicians work in sync,

295
00:19:50,920 --> 00:19:55,180
double-checking measurements, quality,

296
00:19:55,181 --> 00:19:56,440
and performance.

297
00:19:58,020 --> 00:20:00,280
The grafts processed here might be used

298
00:20:00,281 --> 00:20:02,660
for such diverse operations as spinal fusions,

299
00:20:04,760 --> 00:20:07,540
dental implants, and knee repair.

300
00:20:09,080 --> 00:20:11,580
By far, the most common use of tissue grafts

301
00:20:11,581 --> 00:20:13,340
is for sports-related injuries.

302
00:20:14,880 --> 00:20:17,680
Probably the largest growth segment that we have

303
00:20:17,681 --> 00:20:19,320
as an industry in sports medicine.

304
00:20:19,980 --> 00:20:22,560
As the baby boomers like myself are getting older

305
00:20:22,561 --> 00:20:24,940
and being weekend warriors, we're tearing up everything.

306
00:20:25,180 --> 00:20:34,740
It takes approximately 90 days for the tissue from one donor to be fully processed, packaged,

307
00:20:34,741 --> 00:20:47,620
and shipped out, from small boxes to liquid nitrogen containers.

308
00:20:47,621 --> 00:20:53,180
The orders keep the packaging and shipping departments busy 24-7.

309
00:20:53,181 --> 00:21:00,180
Within the last 25 years, the amount of tissue in the body that can be donated and recycled

310
00:21:00,181 --> 00:21:05,520
has more than tripled.

311
00:21:05,521 --> 00:21:13,560
While some of the dead save and improve lives through organ and tissue donation, others

312
00:21:13,561 --> 00:21:17,460
save lives through education.

313
00:21:17,461 --> 00:21:23,160
For the last 40 years, the University of Toledo has run a body donation program that allows

314
00:21:23,161 --> 00:21:26,720
medical students the privilege to learn about the body from the outside in.

315
00:21:29,120 --> 00:21:34,340
I have the utmost respect for people that donate their bodies because they are giving us

316
00:21:34,341 --> 00:21:36,806
the ultimate gift of learning.

317
00:21:36,807 --> 00:21:41,820
So can someone show me the surface landmark for the clavicle?

318
00:21:41,821 --> 00:21:48,360
You can read in a book, you can look at countless pictures, but there's absolutely no substitute

319
00:21:48,361 --> 00:21:53,620
to the hands-on learning that you get from dissecting a cadaver.

320
00:21:53,920 --> 00:21:57,140
It helped me mature as a medical student.

321
00:21:58,620 --> 00:22:01,880
We use the term cadaver, which is someone who's dead,

322
00:22:02,240 --> 00:22:05,020
but in my mind, a cadaver is someone who's more than that,

323
00:22:05,180 --> 00:22:09,300
someone here who's made the decision to give their body for instruction

324
00:22:09,301 --> 00:22:12,220
so that our students can really get some value

325
00:22:12,221 --> 00:22:15,820
out of what really is just the remnant of their earthly self.

326
00:22:15,821 --> 00:22:20,060
Medical cadavers contain no blood,

327
00:22:20,580 --> 00:22:22,920
as they are specially embalmed for lengthy preservation.

328
00:22:24,720 --> 00:22:29,300
This process enables students to see and feel what goes on inside the body.

329
00:22:31,100 --> 00:22:35,660
A cadaveric dissection is for the purpose of exploring the entire human body,

330
00:22:36,140 --> 00:22:40,800
from superficial to deep, examining all systems of the body,

331
00:22:40,801 --> 00:22:45,480
but really from the perspective of students learning it for the first time

332
00:22:45,481 --> 00:22:47,980
or for review purposes.

333
00:22:47,981 --> 00:22:50,980
And if people are willing to donate, that's awesome.

334
00:22:50,981 --> 00:22:52,820
I know I'm planning on donating my body

335
00:22:52,821 --> 00:22:54,420
after going through this experience.

336
00:22:54,421 --> 00:22:59,520
Just giving back to science, which is going to be our careers,

337
00:22:59,521 --> 00:23:00,820
I think is excellent.

338
00:23:02,960 --> 00:23:05,340
Thanks to the dead, researchers here

339
00:23:05,341 --> 00:23:08,340
were able to create valuable virtual education software.

340
00:23:10,800 --> 00:23:14,180
Three of us, anatomists and a medical illustrator,

341
00:23:14,181 --> 00:23:20,340
have developed a software product which can be used very nicely to supplement or enhance

342
00:23:20,341 --> 00:23:23,940
the study of cadaver dissection.

343
00:23:23,941 --> 00:23:29,780
Over a decade in the making, their software called Anatomy and Physiology Revealed

344
00:23:29,781 --> 00:23:35,780
contains thousands of images of the human body presented in a whole new way.

345
00:23:35,781 --> 00:23:40,740
Each anatomical layer from more than 125 different dissections was photographed

346
00:23:40,741 --> 00:23:42,860
then stacked and realigned on the computer.

347
00:23:46,160 --> 00:23:50,340
The program allows successive layers of anatomy to be melted away.

348
00:23:52,240 --> 00:23:55,180
Skin becomes transparent to reveal muscles,

349
00:23:55,680 --> 00:23:58,820
which can be melted away to reveal deeper structures.

350
00:24:00,660 --> 00:24:05,400
Today what you'll do is palpate the clavicles and the notch between them.

351
00:24:05,401 --> 00:24:09,400
Though groundbreaking, this software doesn't eliminate the need to work with the real thing.

352
00:24:10,740 --> 00:24:12,080
It was a little scary.

353
00:24:12,220 --> 00:24:12,920
It was kind of surreal.

354
00:24:13,160 --> 00:24:14,880
You know, the first time you're, you know,

355
00:24:14,920 --> 00:24:17,060
cutting into, you know, a real person.

356
00:24:18,840 --> 00:24:22,420
But it's a really cool experience, too.

357
00:24:24,060 --> 00:24:27,300
More than 5,000 corpses are donated to medical schools

358
00:24:27,301 --> 00:24:28,780
in the United States each year.

359
00:24:29,540 --> 00:24:32,120
But this figure is dwarfed by the tens of thousands

360
00:24:32,121 --> 00:24:33,740
of corpses of non-donors.

361
00:24:35,540 --> 00:24:39,020
Still, donors and non-donors alike can use the services

362
00:24:39,021 --> 00:24:42,580
of the massive industry that houses and prepares them

363
00:24:42,581 --> 00:24:47,520
for their final journey, which could be six feet under.

364
00:24:49,460 --> 00:24:52,400
In the last 20 years, there have been more than 10 million

365
00:24:52,401 --> 00:24:53,480
tissue transplants.

366
00:24:54,020 --> 00:24:57,480
In the last four decades, half a million cornea transplants.

367
00:24:58,200 --> 00:25:00,400
Corpse Tech will return on Modern Marvels.

368
00:25:04,540 --> 00:25:06,660
Death is inevitable for all of us,

369
00:25:06,661 --> 00:25:11,660
and we cope with its sobering finality, with rituals.

370
00:25:11,760 --> 00:25:14,660
Man is not truly that rational,

371
00:25:14,760 --> 00:25:19,700
and death is one of those things that we cannot even express

372
00:25:19,800 --> 00:25:22,680
in verbal form, so we surround it with tradition.

373
00:25:24,740 --> 00:25:26,120
In the Western world,

374
00:25:26,220 --> 00:25:28,740
a business with almost 200 years of tradition

375
00:25:28,840 --> 00:25:30,380
is the funeral home.

376
00:25:32,320 --> 00:25:34,360
Here, morticians can give the illusion

377
00:25:34,361 --> 00:25:38,500
give the illusion of holding death at bay.

378
00:25:38,501 --> 00:25:42,740
The primary component in this practice is embalming.

379
00:25:42,741 --> 00:25:46,640
The embalming process is one in which we really

380
00:25:46,641 --> 00:25:51,080
create an artificial circulation system.

381
00:25:51,081 --> 00:25:53,080
Embalming fluid is the main ingredient

382
00:25:53,081 --> 00:25:58,520
in this unique amalgamation of technology and tradition.

383
00:25:58,521 --> 00:26:03,060
The process is circulatory, but we don't have the heart,

384
00:26:03,061 --> 00:26:06,300
So what we need is a machine to produce the pressure

385
00:26:06,301 --> 00:26:10,540
within the arteries so that we can push the blood out

386
00:26:10,541 --> 00:26:12,540
and put the embalming fluid in.

387
00:26:12,541 --> 00:26:15,940
And that's done by what they call an embalming machine.

388
00:26:15,941 --> 00:26:22,020
And the embalming machine essentially creates

389
00:26:22,021 --> 00:26:27,380
the same type of situation that you would have

390
00:26:27,381 --> 00:26:28,460
with a heart pumping.

391
00:26:28,461 --> 00:26:36,540
The hose of the machine is most often inserted into the common carotid artery in the neck.

392
00:26:38,940 --> 00:26:44,120
The blood is replaced with the chemical fluid as the pumping of the embalming machine pushes

393
00:26:44,121 --> 00:26:49,000
the corpse's blood through the entire body and out through an incision in the jugular.

394
00:26:51,120 --> 00:26:56,600
Nearly 10 pints of blood, the average for an adult male, are simply flushed down the drain.

395
00:26:58,460 --> 00:27:05,500
In ancient Peru and Egypt, spices and oils were used to help preserve the body in a mummified state.

396
00:27:09,640 --> 00:27:15,220
During the Crusades, European apothecaries experimented with preservative recipes.

397
00:27:19,240 --> 00:27:24,640
And during America's civil war, embalming came to the attention of a skeptical public.

398
00:27:24,641 --> 00:27:29,640
Arsenic and formaldehyde allowed medical doctors to successfully

399
00:27:29,641 --> 00:27:35,640
embalm and transport dead soldiers great distances back to their families for burial.

400
00:27:35,641 --> 00:27:43,640
When Abraham Lincoln was embalmed, Americans embraced the practice as both sanitary and civilized.

401
00:27:43,641 --> 00:27:49,640
But perhaps the greatest early example of a successful embalming was in 1924,

402
00:27:49,641 --> 00:27:52,640
with the demise of Vladimir Lenin.

403
00:27:52,641 --> 00:27:55,020
Lenin.

404
00:27:55,021 --> 00:27:58,640
Doctors and scientists devised a powerful concoction of chemicals

405
00:27:58,641 --> 00:28:02,700
to preserve the Russian Revolutionary.

406
00:28:02,701 --> 00:28:04,960
Lenin's corpse is still on permanent view

407
00:28:04,961 --> 00:28:07,820
at his mausoleum in Moscow, although every year or so

408
00:28:07,821 --> 00:28:09,020
he gets a little primping.

409
00:28:11,760 --> 00:28:14,540
As much as modern chemicals can preserve a body,

410
00:28:14,541 --> 00:28:18,600
so too can certain geographic locations.

411
00:28:18,601 --> 00:28:21,840
In the peat bogs of Denmark, the most well-preserved body

412
00:28:21,841 --> 00:28:27,060
from prehistoric times, was discovered in 1950.

413
00:28:27,061 --> 00:28:29,560
This 4th century BC farmer was preserved

414
00:28:29,561 --> 00:28:32,400
by acidic bog water that essentially acted

415
00:28:32,401 --> 00:28:33,940
as a pickling agent on the body.

416
00:28:37,820 --> 00:28:41,540
The acid makes it impossible for any bacteria to grow.

417
00:28:41,541 --> 00:28:46,120
Therefore, organic material doesn't decompose.

418
00:28:46,121 --> 00:28:49,580
But as good as this naturally preserved corpse looks,

419
00:28:49,581 --> 00:28:51,100
it probably wouldn't pass muster

420
00:28:51,101 --> 00:28:54,200
with a modern mortician.

421
00:28:54,201 --> 00:28:56,900
Today, morticians like Nathan Bittner

422
00:28:56,901 --> 00:28:59,900
are skilled at what is called restorative art

423
00:28:59,901 --> 00:29:03,480
that he demonstrates with a student volunteer.

424
00:29:03,481 --> 00:29:05,040
This gentleman here, he certainly

425
00:29:05,041 --> 00:29:10,160
has more color than a corpse that we normally work with.

426
00:29:10,161 --> 00:29:13,260
So what I'll do is I'll try to make everything look even.

427
00:29:13,261 --> 00:29:16,360
What I'm first going to do is try to figure out a color that

428
00:29:16,361 --> 00:29:17,500
will help blend evenly.

429
00:29:17,501 --> 00:29:22,500
And of course, we'll have to do the whole, his whole fingers,

430
00:29:22,501 --> 00:29:23,500
his whole hands.

431
00:29:23,501 --> 00:29:26,500
His hands will be viewable, and we'll certainly

432
00:29:26,501 --> 00:29:27,500
do his face as well.

433
00:29:27,501 --> 00:29:29,500
We want to try to make sure the face and the hands

434
00:29:29,501 --> 00:29:31,500
are consistent in color.

435
00:29:31,501 --> 00:29:35,500
As in any profession, it helps that a funeral director

436
00:29:35,501 --> 00:29:37,500
takes pride in his work.

437
00:29:37,501 --> 00:29:43,500
When it comes to the deceased, there really are canvas.

438
00:29:43,501 --> 00:29:47,100
So when it comes to being able to cosmetize correctly,

439
00:29:47,200 --> 00:29:49,100
we're going to want to make it look uniform

440
00:29:49,200 --> 00:29:52,700
and to make it look as natural as possible.

441
00:29:52,800 --> 00:29:54,740
And let's not forget the most symbolic

442
00:29:54,840 --> 00:29:58,240
and perhaps most necessary of all funeral accoutrements,

443
00:29:58,340 --> 00:30:01,160
the casket.

444
00:30:01,260 --> 00:30:05,260
Here is where choices abound, ranging from plain pine boxes

445
00:30:05,360 --> 00:30:09,260
to solid bronze coffins that will seal your fate.

446
00:30:09,360 --> 00:30:12,400
More than 1.7 million are buried annually.

447
00:30:12,401 --> 00:30:16,300
annually.

448
00:30:16,400 --> 00:30:20,300
The trappings of tradition might cost you a pretty penny.

449
00:30:20,400 --> 00:30:22,740
From the funeral home costs, to a casket,

450
00:30:22,840 --> 00:30:26,440
to a cemetery plot, an average US funeral runs anywhere

451
00:30:26,540 --> 00:30:30,760
from $4,000 to $10,000.

452
00:30:30,860 --> 00:30:33,860
Therefore, you might choose to look to another final ritual

453
00:30:33,960 --> 00:30:39,800
as an alternative, albeit a fiery one.

454
00:30:39,900 --> 00:30:42,000
Murdered civil rights activist Medgar Evers

455
00:30:42,001 --> 00:30:45,700
was so well preserved by embalming in 1963

456
00:30:45,800 --> 00:30:48,540
that an autopsy performed in 1991

457
00:30:48,640 --> 00:30:51,440
helped to finally secure the conviction of his killer.

458
00:30:51,540 --> 00:30:53,740
Corpse Tech will return on Modern Marvels.

459
00:30:58,960 --> 00:31:03,460
United States cemeteries cover millions of acres of land,

460
00:31:03,560 --> 00:31:07,100
often valuable land.

461
00:31:07,200 --> 00:31:10,600
So what's a body to do?

462
00:31:10,601 --> 00:31:15,840
Each year, almost 800,000 Americans will choose cremation over a cemetery burial.

463
00:31:17,660 --> 00:31:20,220
Projections suggest that by the year 2025,

464
00:31:21,020 --> 00:31:23,640
close to 2 million corpses will be cremated every year.

465
00:31:24,560 --> 00:31:32,140
The cremation process is one where the body is reduced to its elements

466
00:31:32,141 --> 00:31:35,800
through flame, heat, and vaporization.

467
00:31:35,801 --> 00:31:40,800
Facultative Technologies, headquartered in Medina, Ohio,

468
00:31:40,801 --> 00:31:44,800
is the leading U.S. manufacturer of cremators.

469
00:31:44,801 --> 00:31:49,800
In the last 25 years, there has been a significant increase

470
00:31:49,801 --> 00:31:54,800
in the consumer's demand and request for cremation.

471
00:31:54,801 --> 00:32:00,800
The cremator is essentially an oven designed to facilitate the combustion process.

472
00:32:00,801 --> 00:32:04,800
While this might sound simple, it's anything but.

473
00:32:04,801 --> 00:32:09,300
There are two chambers inside the cremator.

474
00:32:09,301 --> 00:32:12,800
The primary chamber is where the combustion of the body

475
00:32:12,801 --> 00:32:14,800
and its container or wrappings takes place.

476
00:32:14,801 --> 00:32:18,800
This combustion emits gases and particulate matter,

477
00:32:18,801 --> 00:32:21,800
such as ash and carbon monoxide,

478
00:32:21,801 --> 00:32:24,800
which travel to the secondary chamber,

479
00:32:24,801 --> 00:32:28,800
where final incineration occurs at 1,800 degrees Fahrenheit.

480
00:32:28,801 --> 00:32:31,800
Evidence of an incomplete combustion process

481
00:32:31,801 --> 00:32:36,800
complete combustion process is what you typically see when you see smoke.

482
00:32:36,801 --> 00:32:41,560
In a perfect environment where you control the temperature, the oxygen concentration

483
00:32:41,561 --> 00:32:47,600
with that fuel, there would be no visible evidence that a combustion process was taking

484
00:32:47,601 --> 00:32:53,640
place other than perhaps a radiation of some heat waves that you would see.

485
00:32:53,641 --> 00:32:57,900
That's what Facultative Technologies has claimed to accomplish with its new state-of-the-art

486
00:32:57,901 --> 00:33:01,760
FT3 cremator.

487
00:33:01,761 --> 00:33:03,800
If you're in the market for one, it could set you back

488
00:33:03,900 --> 00:33:08,140
a cool $180,000.

489
00:33:08,240 --> 00:33:09,400
This is the bottom chamber.

490
00:33:09,500 --> 00:33:12,000
We commonly call it the secondary combustion chamber.

491
00:33:12,100 --> 00:33:15,440
And here you see the brickwork in place to the level where we

492
00:33:15,540 --> 00:33:19,740
would now start putting in the primary combustion chamber.

493
00:33:19,840 --> 00:33:22,420
This machine over here now has had the primary combustion

494
00:33:22,520 --> 00:33:24,280
chamber virtually completed.

495
00:33:24,380 --> 00:33:27,480
These tiles here are the hearth tiles where the casket would

496
00:33:27,580 --> 00:33:29,480
sit on for cremation.

497
00:33:29,481 --> 00:33:33,920
And this machine over here, we've got to the point now

498
00:33:33,921 --> 00:33:36,620
where Chuck, our master bricklayer here,

499
00:33:36,621 --> 00:33:40,320
is repointing the brickwork for the arch.

500
00:33:40,321 --> 00:33:43,920
I've been doing this for two years now.

501
00:33:43,921 --> 00:33:45,660
Just another day.

502
00:33:45,661 --> 00:33:46,560
It's a clean machine.

503
00:33:46,561 --> 00:33:47,261
It's new.

504
00:33:47,262 --> 00:33:50,440
It won't bother you.

505
00:33:50,441 --> 00:33:52,700
The brick itself has been specifically designed

506
00:33:52,701 --> 00:33:55,180
by ceramics engineers to withstand

507
00:33:55,181 --> 00:33:58,140
extremely high temperatures.

508
00:33:58,141 --> 00:33:59,480
Well, we have different types of brick.

509
00:33:59,481 --> 00:34:04,160
different thicknesses, obviously, but generally there's over 1,100 bricks in this machine.

510
00:34:04,980 --> 00:34:10,820
And each brick has 42% aluminum content and is capable of going up to 2,300 degrees.

511
00:34:13,000 --> 00:34:17,660
Natural gas fuels the primary chamber, heating it to 1,400 degrees Fahrenheit.

512
00:34:18,860 --> 00:34:22,620
It takes about 20 minutes to reach this temperature for its first run of the day.

513
00:34:22,621 --> 00:34:28,620
And in case you're wondering, that intense heat enables the FT3

514
00:34:28,621 --> 00:34:32,620
cremator to turn a corpse to ash in about 60 minutes.

515
00:34:32,621 --> 00:34:37,620
The cremators made here are not only crafted by hand,

516
00:34:37,621 --> 00:34:41,620
but also custom designed for each client.

517
00:34:41,621 --> 00:34:44,620
We had two cremators here on site,

518
00:34:44,621 --> 00:34:49,620
and we've been using them over the past six to eight years.

519
00:34:49,621 --> 00:34:51,060
to eight years.

520
00:34:51,061 --> 00:34:54,180
But the things that we're finding now with the new technology

521
00:34:54,181 --> 00:34:57,340
is that we're able to not only enhance the speed

522
00:34:57,341 --> 00:35:00,960
of the cremation, but use less natural resources.

523
00:35:00,961 --> 00:35:05,820
And then also, the emissions are much more favorable.

524
00:35:05,821 --> 00:35:07,980
The operator merely presses a button,

525
00:35:07,981 --> 00:35:09,340
and the automated process begins.

526
00:35:12,660 --> 00:35:14,700
The natural gas fire adjusts itself

527
00:35:14,701 --> 00:35:17,260
as it gets information from temperature sensors,

528
00:35:17,261 --> 00:35:20,420
called thermocouples, in the brick walls of both chambers.

529
00:35:22,760 --> 00:35:25,560
An integrated fan system is critical in regulating

530
00:35:25,561 --> 00:35:27,140
the amount of oxygen circulated.

531
00:35:28,420 --> 00:35:30,740
Without oxygen, there would be no flame,

532
00:35:31,340 --> 00:35:32,900
but too much could cause damage.

533
00:35:35,380 --> 00:35:38,280
In this demonstration, an empty wooden casket

534
00:35:38,281 --> 00:35:40,840
is reduced to burning embers in just 20 minutes.

535
00:35:43,260 --> 00:35:46,320
These embers resemble the result of an actual cremation,

536
00:35:46,321 --> 00:35:48,420
commonly called the ashes.

537
00:35:51,360 --> 00:35:54,860
But the term is a misnomer, as they aren't ashes at all,

538
00:35:55,360 --> 00:35:58,840
but bone fragments that will be pulverized into a fine dust.

539
00:36:00,180 --> 00:36:02,420
All the soft tissue has been vaporized.

540
00:36:03,440 --> 00:36:06,680
So the operator will rake all the cremator remains into the ash chute.

541
00:36:07,240 --> 00:36:11,140
This ash chute is a refractory line because the ashes at this particular time are hot.

542
00:36:11,720 --> 00:36:15,460
They would fall down, as you can see here, down to this chute part here,

543
00:36:15,461 --> 00:36:18,160
and it will rest on this cast iron tray here.

544
00:36:19,080 --> 00:36:22,220
This part of the machine is actually a small air plenum

545
00:36:22,221 --> 00:36:26,860
where, once fitted, we inject cold air across the ashes

546
00:36:26,861 --> 00:36:29,700
to actually help the ashes to cool down.

547
00:36:31,420 --> 00:36:35,400
Within two minutes, this machine will pulverize the bone fragments

548
00:36:35,401 --> 00:36:39,080
into granules no larger than 3.2 millimeters in diameter.

549
00:36:39,081 --> 00:36:48,320
When completely ground, the ashes weigh an average of 5 to 6 pounds and are transferred

550
00:36:48,321 --> 00:36:51,640
to an entire family.

551
00:36:51,641 --> 00:36:55,140
Ritual cremation is nothing.

552
00:36:55,141 --> 00:37:00,840
For the last 2,500 years in the ancient city of Oranasi, India, corpses have been set afire

553
00:37:00,841 --> 00:37:06,300
on the banks of the Ganges as part of religious ceremonies.

554
00:37:06,301 --> 00:37:10,200
It takes at least six hours to incinerate one body,

555
00:37:10,201 --> 00:37:13,300
and usually requires burning more than 600 pounds of wood.

556
00:37:15,980 --> 00:37:18,440
Some have estimated that 50 million trees a year

557
00:37:18,441 --> 00:37:20,180
are cut down to provide the wood pyres.

558
00:37:23,420 --> 00:37:27,880
Tens of thousands of cremations occur here every year.

559
00:37:27,881 --> 00:37:30,620
The ashes from each are poured into the Ganges,

560
00:37:30,621 --> 00:37:33,100
where it's believed the soul meets the Hindu gods

561
00:37:33,101 --> 00:37:36,080
in the sacred waters.

562
00:37:36,081 --> 00:37:39,820
Back in the United States, five pounds of ashes also need a home.

563
00:37:41,020 --> 00:37:43,440
So how about this for a final resting place?

564
00:37:47,480 --> 00:37:52,660
Located about three miles off the Miami coast and 45 feet below the ocean surface,

565
00:37:53,480 --> 00:37:57,140
rests this unusual memorial reef maintained by the Neptune Society.

566
00:37:57,141 --> 00:38:03,520
designed as a lost city

567
00:38:03,521 --> 00:38:07,020
it is the largest man-made reef of its kind in the world

568
00:38:07,021 --> 00:38:12,380
it contains more than 125,000 niches for urns

569
00:38:12,381 --> 00:38:17,340
and holds secure the remembrances of the dearly departed for all time

570
00:38:17,341 --> 00:38:23,380
the Neptune Society has created alternative funeral options since 1973

571
00:38:23,381 --> 00:38:27,660
when it offered the choice of scattering the deceased's ashes at sea.

572
00:38:29,200 --> 00:38:34,260
Today, its memorial reef is yet another option for ocean lovers and non-traditionalists.

573
00:38:36,120 --> 00:38:39,720
The reef not only serves as a final resting place for ashes,

574
00:38:40,280 --> 00:38:44,700
but also as an undersea breakwater that reduces erosion,

575
00:38:46,340 --> 00:38:48,060
encourages a thriving marine habitat,

576
00:38:48,061 --> 00:38:51,080
and creates a scuba haven.

577
00:38:51,081 --> 00:38:54,580
But what if cremation isn't for you?

578
00:38:54,581 --> 00:38:57,320
Can a corpse be green?

579
00:38:57,321 --> 00:38:59,320
One company thinks so.

580
00:38:59,321 --> 00:39:05,320
According to a 2006 report, there are just over 2,000

581
00:39:05,321 --> 00:39:07,840
crematories in the United States.

582
00:39:07,841 --> 00:39:11,140
California tops the list with 185.

583
00:39:11,141 --> 00:39:14,640
And North Dakota has the fewest with three.

584
00:39:14,641 --> 00:39:17,640
Corpse Tech will return on Modern Marvels.

585
00:39:21,080 --> 00:39:27,500
Whether it's the 50 million trees for the fuel to feed the funeral pyres of Oronesi,

586
00:39:28,120 --> 00:39:34,480
or the 1.7 million caskets a year that house the dead in the United States,

587
00:39:35,480 --> 00:39:40,620
the business of disposing of corpses is a resource-consuming and costly venture.

588
00:39:42,180 --> 00:39:47,360
Americans pay $20 billion a year to keep the elements at bay from their buried dead.

589
00:39:47,361 --> 00:39:51,160
One cemetery has a different idea.

590
00:39:52,540 --> 00:39:55,100
From the time we're born, we use the earth.

591
00:39:55,340 --> 00:39:56,900
We use it for sustenance.

592
00:39:57,120 --> 00:39:58,540
We feed off the earth.

593
00:39:58,660 --> 00:39:59,440
We breathe the air.

594
00:39:59,840 --> 00:40:02,140
We rely on the sunshine to make everything grow.

595
00:40:02,580 --> 00:40:06,600
It only makes sense that when we go, we can recycle ourselves and give back to the earth

596
00:40:06,601 --> 00:40:10,880
that gave us life in the first place so that the earth can continue to give life to others.

597
00:40:12,220 --> 00:40:15,840
Forever Fernwood, 15 miles outside of San Francisco,

598
00:40:15,841 --> 00:40:19,480
was established in 1892 on 32 acres.

599
00:40:20,980 --> 00:40:25,160
Now it has taken a new approach to the concept of the final resting place.

600
00:40:26,300 --> 00:40:28,660
Natural burial is back to basics.

601
00:40:29,000 --> 00:40:31,860
It's earth to earth, ashes to ashes, dust to dust.

602
00:40:32,300 --> 00:40:36,400
We do natural burial without the use of chemicals like formaldehyde.

603
00:40:36,920 --> 00:40:40,420
If a family doesn't wish to use a casket, they don't even have to,

604
00:40:40,421 --> 00:40:44,180
but if they do, we use 100% biodegradable caskets

605
00:40:44,181 --> 00:40:46,260
made of sustainable woods like pine.

606
00:40:46,820 --> 00:40:48,680
If they don't wish to use a casket,

607
00:40:48,740 --> 00:40:49,940
they could be wrapped in a shroud

608
00:40:49,941 --> 00:40:51,960
and placed directly into the earth.

609
00:40:53,080 --> 00:40:55,460
This is where the dead go green.

610
00:40:56,740 --> 00:40:57,960
Everything that's buried here

611
00:40:57,961 --> 00:41:00,160
will naturally degrade and fertilize the soil.

612
00:41:03,940 --> 00:41:06,540
The natural burial portion of forever fernwood

613
00:41:06,541 --> 00:41:07,940
spans about 20 acres,

614
00:41:08,580 --> 00:41:10,320
enough for more than 8,000 bodies.

615
00:41:10,321 --> 00:41:16,820
This open space feels more like a park than a cemetery.

616
00:41:16,821 --> 00:41:17,820
And that's the idea.

617
00:41:17,821 --> 00:41:23,760
But just because it's all natural doesn't mean 21st century

618
00:41:23,761 --> 00:41:27,560
technology doesn't play a part.

619
00:41:27,561 --> 00:41:30,680
Forever Fernwood has combined the Green Revolution with the

620
00:41:30,681 --> 00:41:35,060
latest in global positioning satellite tools.

621
00:41:35,061 --> 00:41:37,980
Technology has been a great help even when we're dealing

622
00:41:37,981 --> 00:41:39,480
with natural burials.

623
00:41:39,481 --> 00:41:44,880
We're able to locate any grave, we're able to locate any person who's buried using GPS

624
00:41:44,881 --> 00:41:46,480
and GIS technology.

625
00:41:46,481 --> 00:41:53,720
This GPS receiver provides accurate electronic maps for what lies beneath.

626
00:41:53,721 --> 00:41:58,480
Very handy for finding an empty plot or a departed friend.

627
00:41:58,481 --> 00:42:03,600
When we bury somebody in order to be able to locate them later on, we use what's called

628
00:42:03,601 --> 00:42:05,740
a radiofrequency ID tag.

629
00:42:05,741 --> 00:42:07,920
This little pink ball is buried with the person.

630
00:42:07,921 --> 00:42:12,020
the only thing that's not biodegradable that's put there, and that's intentional because we want

631
00:42:12,021 --> 00:42:13,868
this to last for about 500 years.

632
00:42:13,869 --> 00:42:17,060
We then use this apparatus, which I believe the technical

633
00:42:17,061 --> 00:42:18,971
term is the yellow ray gun.

634
00:42:18,972 --> 00:42:23,500
We turn it on, and this is able to locate exactly where this is.

635
00:42:30,260 --> 00:42:34,180
This bulky ray gun will soon be replaced with a PDA-sized version.

636
00:42:34,181 --> 00:42:37,560
that will allow visitors to find their destination with ease.

637
00:42:40,320 --> 00:42:43,720
Forever Fernwood's biggest difference from traditional cemeteries

638
00:42:43,721 --> 00:42:45,760
lies below ground.

639
00:42:49,160 --> 00:42:53,340
For the last eight decades, most cemeteries have required the deceased

640
00:42:53,341 --> 00:42:55,020
to be buried not only in a coffin,

641
00:42:55,480 --> 00:42:58,640
but also that the coffin rests inside a concrete vault,

642
00:42:59,080 --> 00:43:01,520
which provides support for the ground directly above it.

643
00:43:04,180 --> 00:43:09,680
Concrete vaults have been utilized in order to keep a grave from collapsing.

644
00:43:09,681 --> 00:43:13,980
When the weight of the earth presses down on a casket over time, it'll cause that casket

645
00:43:13,981 --> 00:43:18,840
to collapse, which means on the surface you have a big gap, which means the lawn doesn't

646
00:43:18,841 --> 00:43:21,320
look very nice.

647
00:43:21,321 --> 00:43:26,340
But here on the open meadowland, dips, curves and cracks in the soil are barely noticeable

648
00:43:26,341 --> 00:43:27,840
and seem natural.

649
00:43:27,841 --> 00:43:34,180
We have an old world solution when a grave collapses, we just add more dirt to the top.

650
00:43:34,181 --> 00:43:39,180
Technology with sustainability is the motto here.

651
00:43:39,181 --> 00:43:43,180
This natural cemetery is one of very few in the United States,

652
00:43:43,181 --> 00:43:47,180
but at least five more are planned.

653
00:43:47,181 --> 00:43:53,180
Perhaps its success represents how breaking from tradition may not be a bad thing.

654
00:43:53,181 --> 00:43:59,180
With natural burial, not only is there no negative impact, but there's a positive impact.

655
00:43:59,181 --> 00:44:04,120
That tree that's planted on your grave is going to be sucking up all that CO2,

656
00:44:04,121 --> 00:44:07,680
and it's going to be breathing out oxygen for the next century at least.

657
00:44:08,920 --> 00:44:13,500
A simple burial like one at Forever Fernwood may seem odd in today's world,

658
00:44:14,260 --> 00:44:17,440
but it's just another example of an old idea.

